//? Title: Maximum Likelihood and MAP Regression
//? Subtitle: Gaussian coefficient shrinkage with correlated predictors
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

\abstract(
Fit the same Gaussian regression by maximum likelihood and by maximum
a posteriori estimation. Sixteen fitting rows have eight correlated predictors.
A separate test sample shows how the Gaussian coefficient prior changes prediction.
)
\toc

> Likelihood and prior

With known observation variance $\sigma^2$, the Gaussian negative log likelihood
up to an additive constant is $\|y-Xw\|^2/(2\sigma^2)$. An isotropic Gaussian
prior with variance $\tau^2$ adds $\|w\|^2/(2\tau^2)$ to the negative log
posterior. Its mode is the same as its mean and solves the ridge system
$(X^\top X+(\sigma^2/\tau^2)I)w=X^\top y$.

The predictors share a large common component. The likelihood can fit that
component while remaining weakly informative about individual coefficients.
The Gaussian prior shrinks coefficients toward zero. This experiment measures
the likelihood, posterior objective, coefficient norm and prediction error
separately. The design omits an intercept and applies the prior to all eight
coefficients.

> Fit and evaluate

The source generator uses one shared standard normal draw per row and adds
independent noise at scale .25 to each predictor. The response noise has
standard deviation .6. The coefficients alternate between .45 and -.25.
The two fits share 16 generated observations and are scored on the same
256 independently generated rows. The MAP fit uses prior SD .35.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/mle_map_fit.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  constexpr int features = 8;
  constexpr double noise_sd = .6, prior_sd = .35;
  const auto sample = [](int rows, unsigned seed) {
    std::mt19937 generator(seed);
    std::normal_distribution<double> normal;
    std::vector<double> design, response;
    for (int row = 0; row < rows; ++row) {
      const double shared = normal(generator);
      double y = noise_sd * normal(generator);
      for (int col = 0; col < features; ++col) {
        const double x = shared + .25 * normal(generator);
        design.push_back(x);
        y += x * (col % 2 == 0 ? .45 : -.25);
      }
      response.push_back(y);
    }
    return std::pair{n::from_vector(design, {rows, features}),
                     n::from_vector(response, {rows, 1})};
  };
  const auto [x, y] = sample(16, 66213);
  const auto [test_x, test_y] = sample(256, 66214);
  const auto mle = n::ml::LeastSquaresQr(x, y);
  const auto posterior = n::ml::FitBayesianLinear(x, y, noise_sd * noise_sd, prior_sd * prior_sd);
  const auto map = posterior.mean;
  const auto mle_prediction = n::matmul(test_x, mle);
  const auto map_prediction = n::ml::PredictBayesianLinear(posterior, test_x).mean.reshape({256, 1});

  const auto metrics = [&](const n::Tensor &coefficients, const n::Tensor &prediction) {
    const double nll = n::sum(n::square(y - n::matmul(x, coefficients))).item() /
                       (2 * noise_sd * noise_sd);
    const double norm = n::sum(n::square(coefficients)).item();
    const double rmse = n::sqrt(n::mean(n::square(test_y - prediction))).item();
    return std::vector<double>{nll, nll + norm / (2 * prior_sd * prior_sd), norm, rmse};
  };
  const auto a = metrics(mle, mle_prediction), b = metrics(map, map_prediction);
  if (!(a[0] < b[0] && b[1] < a[1] && b[2] < a[2] &&
        std::isfinite(a[3]) && std::isfinite(b[3])))
    throw std::runtime_error("Gaussian likelihood and prior checks failed");

  const auto feature = n::arange(1, 9).to_vector<double>();
  auto parameters = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  parameters.lines(feature, mle.to_vector<double>(), "MLE", {185, 28, 28})
      .lines(feature, map.to_vector<double>(), "MAP", {37, 99, 235})
      .abline_h(0.).title("Coefficient estimates").x_label("predictor").y_label("weight");
  const auto observed = test_y.to_vector<double>();
  const double lo = n::min(test_y).item(), hi = n::max(test_y).item();
  auto fitted = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  fitted.lines({lo, hi}, {lo, hi}, "identity", {150, 150, 150})
      .points(observed, mle_prediction.to_vector<double>(), "MLE", dm::RGBA{185, 28, 28, .4})
      .points(observed, map_prediction.to_vector<double>(), "MAP", dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235, .4})
      .title("Predictions on 256 independent rows").x_label("observed").y_label("predicted");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(parameters).add(fitted).fit_panels()
      .size(1100, 465).save_svg("build/mle_map_fit.svg");
  n::save_npz("build/mle_map_regression.npz", {{"train_x", x}, {"train_y", y},
      {"test_x", test_x}, {"test_y", test_y}, {"mle", mle}, {"map", map},
      {"mle_metrics", n::from_vector(a)}, {"map_metrics", n::from_vector(b)}});

  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("method", std::vector<std::string>{"MLE", "MAP"});
  table.add_column("negative log likelihood", std::vector<double>{a[0], b[0]});
  table.add_column("negative log posterior", std::vector<double>{a[1], b[1]});
  table.add_column("squared coefficient norm", std::vector<double>{a[2], b[2]});
  table.add_column("test RMSE", std::vector<double>{a[3], b[3]});
  std::print("{}", table.to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | negative log likelihood | negative log posterior | squared coefficient norm | test RMSE |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| MLE | 3.386365 | 14.291094 | 2.671659 | 0.764283 |
| MAP | 5.231199 | 6.257966 | 0.251558 | 0.677850 |
\image(figures/mle_map_fit.svg)
// result_end
\alttext(The MAP coefficients stay closer to zero. The second panel compares both sets of predictions against the observed test values.)

The unpenalized fit has the lower training negative log likelihood. The MAP
fit has the lower negative log posterior and smaller coefficient norm. Its
lower test error in this experiment reflects the small fitting sample and
correlated design.

> References

- [Rasmussen and Williams, Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning, chapter 2](https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW2.pdf).
